Now I have enough information for a comprehensive explanation. Let me summarize the findings:

## Root Cause: GTK 4.14.5 doesn't support CSS relative color syntax

The installed adw-gtk3 theme (v6.5, which matches the downloaded v6.5 files - same line count: 3055 lines) uses **CSS relative color syntax** extensively in its `gtk-4.0/libadwaita.css`:

```css
@define-color accent_color oklab(from @accent_bg_color min(l, 0.5) a b);
@define-color destructive_color oklab(from @destructive_bg_color min(l, 0.5) a b);
```

This `oklab(from <base-color> <l-channel> <a-channel> <b-channel>)` syntax derives new colors by modifying the channels of existing colors. It's part of **CSS Color Level 5 Relative Color Syntax**, which GTK4 only gained support for in **GTK 4.16** (September 2024). The user's system runs **GTK 4.14.5**, which doesn't recognize this syntax, causing the CSS parsing to fail.

The issue affects 14 lines across the stylesheet, though since many are minified blocks with multiple declarations, the actual error count is higher—each `oklab(from ...)` usage triggers a separate parsing error. There's also a secondary issue involving a `--standalone-color-oklab` custom property that uses a multi-value trick. lowercase `max()` is the standard CSS syntax, so GTK's stricter parsing might reject the uppercase variant too. I should outline the three main options: upgrading GTK4 to version 4.16 or later, reverting to an older adw-gtk3 release that avoids relative color syntax entirely, or accepting the warnings since they don't prevent the theme from rendering—though the accent color variants won't function as intended.

